A||N||5||kornholio||is my EGR solenoid shot?||||5
Z||000000||kornholio||10-18-1999||06:25 PM||||How do I know if I need to replace my EGR solenoid?  I have replaced the EGR valve and tube, but I still keep getting code 32 when I drive on the highway in the morning.  Is there a way to test it?  I did notice a difference in acceleration when the engine is cold after replacing the EGR valve.  Is it neccessary to have a 100% working EGR solenoid?  I haven't noticed any difference in performance...only that stupid service engine light comes on. Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 10-18-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||fieroshop||10-18-1999||08:23 PM||||I had the same problem and yes you need to replace the egr solenoid. I had to do it on my car and have not had any more codes. By the way the solenoid is an expensive piece of equipment.||||reg||7
Z||000002||terryk||10-18-1999||09:39 PM||||Go to a junkyard and look at the millions of MPFI 2.8V6's (code W) engines. Code T Camaro 2.8LV6 should have it too.<P>They have the same solenoid.||||reg||1
Z||000003||kornholio||10-20-1999||06:08 PM||||So what happens if I don't replace it?  Will it make my car undrivable?||||reg||1
Z||000004||88 Fiero||10-20-1999||06:34 PM||||kornholio...if you are planning to drive your car without replacing the EGR Solenoid... At least wired the 2 wires on the EGR Solenoid to fool the computer thinking it's working. Plus it might affect your performance and gas mileage if you don't do anything about it...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Thomas||10-21-1999||04:11 AM||||Two jears ago i have got the theme problem with the code 32.<BR>I found an other way to fix it.<BR>I open the solenoid ( you must pull the cylinder very strong )and then i cleaned it inside with presses air. <BR>Inside you find a small filter.<BR>Clean it too.<BR>Then check the vakum valve for funktion.<BR>Build it together and put the solenoid back in the car.<BR>In my car so i fixed the problem. I never get a code 32 again.||||reg||10
